<Project>

  <!--
    Repo-specific signing customization, imported automatically by Arcade's
    toolset Sign.props (see $(RepositoryEngineeringDir)Signing.props in
    src/Microsoft.DotNet.Arcade.Sdk/toolset/Sign.props).

    No customization is needed for the ItemsToSign discovery itself: Arcade's
    default (EnableDefaultArtifacts=true, EnableDefaultRidSpecificArtifacts
    unset) already picks up every *.nupkg under $(ArtifactsPackagesDir)**,
    which includes both ZeroGC packaging projects'
    Microsoft.DotNet.RuntimeLab.ZeroGC.Net10/Net11 .nupkgs automatically.
    Its default FileExtensionSignInfo also already maps ".dll" ->
    "Microsoft400" and ".nupkg" -> "NuGet", so the embedded win-x64
    ZeroGC.dll and the containing package are both signed with no extra
    wiring here.

    What Arcade's default does NOT cover is Linux's libZeroGC.so: there is
    no built-in FileExtensionSignInfo entry for ".so" (unlike ".dylib",
    which maps to "MacDeveloperHarden" for macOS notarization). Microsoft's
    signing infrastructure does not Authenticode-sign raw ELF shared
    objects - only the containing .nupkg gets a NuGet signature, same as
    dotnet/runtime's own native Linux assets. Declare this explicitly
    (CertificateName="None") so SignTool treats it as a deliberate,
    documented no-op rather than an unmapped-extension warning.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <FileExtensionSignInfo Include=".so" CertificateName="None" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
